course: dictionary
lesson: 6477
title: Naught: nothing, and zero
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Naught is an old word that means nothing. You will meet it in old books, poems and a few set phrases. Context helps you spot it. «Naughty» comes from «naught», so the two words are related.
explain: Look for the clues. «Come to naught» means fail, come to nothing. «All for naught» means for nothing, wasted. Test your meaning by swapping it in: "all for nothing" still makes sense, so naught means nothing.
explain: Naught can also mean zero, the number 0, mainly in US English. In the UK, the number 0 is usually written nought. Check the text to see which meaning fits.
fact: Naught means nothing. It is old-fashioned and mostly found in literature.
fact: «Naughty» comes from «naught»: it first meant having nothing, then wicked, and now mostly a child behaving badly.
fact: «Come to naught» means fail or come to nothing.
fact: «All for naught» means for nothing, so the effort was wasted.
fact: Naught can also mean zero, the number 0, mainly in US English. UK English usually writes «nought» for zero.
exercise: «All their hard work came to naught.» What does come to naught mean? | *Fail, come to nothing | Get a prize | Grow very big | Arrive early
exercise: Which everyday word comes from naught? | *Naughty | Knight | Nightly | Notice
exercise: In a UK maths book, how is the number 0 usually written? | Nowt | Zilch | *Nought | Naught
test: «He spent years on the plan, but it all came to naught.» What does naught mean here? | *Nothing, so the plan failed | A short rest | Lots of money | A great success
test: Which phrase means wasted, done for nothing? | Come to a head | *All for naught | Tried all day | Naughty behaviour
exam: In the phrase «all for naught», what does naught mean? | A reward for good work | A small amount of time | *Nothing, for no reason | Money given back
